Package: RcppBandicoot
Title: 'Rcpp' Integration for the 'Bandicoot' Header-Only C++ GPU Accelerated Linear Algebra Library
Version: 4.0.2.1
Authors@R: c(person("James Joseph", "Balamuta", email = "balamut2@illinois.edu", 
                    role = c("aut", "cre", "cph"), 
                    comment = c(ORCID = "0000-0003-2826-8458")),
             person("Dirk", "Eddelbuettel", role = c("aut"), email = "edd@debian.org",
                    comment = c(ORCID = "0000-0001-6419-907X")))       
Description: 'Bandicoot' is a templated C++ GPU Linear Algebra library
 (by the 'Armadillo' team) that provides a simple set of abstractions for writing
 high performant code for graphics processing units.
 The 'RcppBandicoot' package includes the header files from the 'Bandicoot' library.
 Therefore, users do not need to install 'Bandicoot' to use 'RcppBandicoot'.
 Note that the 'Bandicoot' library is licensed under Apache License version 2.0 and
 'RcppBandicoot' (the 'Rcpp' bindings/bridge to 'Bandicoot') is licensed under
 the GNU GPL version 2 or later. Building 'RcppBandicoot' requires the
 development files for 'OpenCL' (>= 1.2) or 'CUDA'; running a computation on a
 device additionally requires a GPU with working vendor drivers.
License: GPL (>= 2)
URL: https://github.com/coatless-rpkg/rcppbandicoot
BugReports: https://github.com/coatless-rpkg/rcppbandicoot/issues
Depends: R (>= 4.2.3)
LinkingTo: Rcpp (>= 1.1.0)
Imports: Rcpp (>= 1.1.0)
Suggests: testthat (>= 3.0.0)
Config/testthat/edition: 3
SystemRequirements: To build: OpenCL headers and an OpenCL ICD loader (Debian
 and Ubuntu: opencl-headers, ocl-icd-opencl-dev; Fedora, RHEL and derivatives:
 opencl-headers, ocl-icd; openSUSE: opencl-headers, libOpenCL1; Alpine:
 opencl-dev; macOS: the OpenCL framework shipped with the system), or, for the
 optional CUDA backend, the CUDA toolkit (cuda, cudart, cuBLAS, cuRAND,
 cuSOLVER and NVRTC). Optionally CLBlast (recommended) or clBLAS, which
 accelerate the OpenCL BLAS calls. To run a computation: a GPU device with
 working vendor drivers. Headers and an ICD loader alone -- which is all the
 CRAN and r-universe build machines install -- enumerate zero devices, so
 binaries built there load fine but report no usable device.
